 @gorkskoal9315  It depends. Intel's GPU here seems to be getting hit hard by driver limitations, similar to how AMD's older GPUs were, albeit differently because the issue is mostly an issue with older APIs/games. Nevertheless, this GPU is a great showing for a first step into the dGPU market.

Your point about the systems relying on synchronous operations is a bit confusing, as the examples you give like texture and player pop in are not due to being unable to queue up textures and models from a driver level, and more of an issue with optimization. Most developers are trying to work with constraints such as low VRAM and/or slow HDDs when trying to figure out how to optimize their texture loading procedures. Sure you can preload textures on the VRAM or even RAM if you really wanted to, but not everyone has a system that can keep up. This is why Microsoft came up with DirectStorage--you can stream assets directly from SSDs instead of going to the CPU first to load assets because with the way texture sizes are increasing, even 32GB of RAM isn't going to save you. Though, even that won't completely eliminate pop in since not everyone uses SSDs in the first place, especially not fast ones.

Anyways, let's hope that driver updates do fix the card's performance with older APIs (DX9,DX11, OpenGL?). It would make the A380 a solid entry to the budget market, especially with that hardware AV1 encoder.

