 @saricubra2867  OpenGL and Vulkan are completely different designs, OpenGL was designed for rendering in software, and slowly adapted to hardware over 20 years, OpenGL uses a state-pipeline, and DX a COM model, OpenGL was superior in the early Quake days, but it's state-machine was really hard to thread on the CPU and required some serious hacks to multithread it, AMD never had a multithreaded system and was a lot slower than Nvidia for many years, DX11 had a superior design for quite a while for multi-threading.

And then eventually in 2013 Nvidia/OpenGL came out with AZDO features that made multithreading more explicit, that made it even better than DX11, but by that time AMD had their Mantle API which was rumored to be basically inspired by Xbox pre-release DX12, which then AMD gave to the Khronos group, and renamed Vulkan. Both DX12 and Vulkan have more in common with each other than OpenGL or DX11, because they're designed to program modern GPUs, not to draw triangles easily.

tl;dr: <=DX11 and OpenGL are managed APIs that come with 20 years of baggage, Vulkan and DX12 are about exposing hardware to engines with no management and no assumptions.

