 @vsytch  DMA copies are not the way things usually happen anyway; the buffers that you receive in DX12/Vulkan are generally (unless requested; and this is avoided in most engines) not in the main RAM, they're memory-mapped and you're writing to them into VRAM through the logical PCI bus. There are of course intricacies of when the memory mapped becomes actually available for the driver to bind and how much of a stall it causes in the driver; and also how the memory is mapped, what its cache behaviour is.

The way it was in earlier APIs, a lot of things, textures particularly, were in main RAM and were fetched by the GPU via Bus Master, which you could say is a DMA mechanism.

Both are the things that Intel didn't have to deal with before, because it would just allocate things in main memory and access them via a side channel to the memory controller, since the CPU and the GPU would share the die and the memory controller, logical PCI would only be for scarce control information.

